d/ts have open issues, so I shouldn't create duplicates. But I'm wondering what gaps remain—maybe tests for these components, or other aspects not yet covered by the existing issues.\n\nActually, rereading the planning guidelines about splitting implementation from tests, I see the existing issues handle the core implementations, but there's a separate requirement for differential tests on cmd/ts that use the testing harness. Looking at the requirements, those tests need to cover multiple modes and formats, which suggests that's the missing piece for rel01.0 completion. The next logical task is implementing differential tests for cmd/ts that verify the Go implementation against the reference binary, which depends on both the testutils package and the cmd/ts implementation itself. This would complete the rel01.0 use case by adding the R9 requirement coverage, and should fit comfortably within the line budget as a test file with around 13 test cases. R2: Apply TimestampNormalizer from pkg/testutils for tests with wall-clock timestamps\n- R3: Cover: default format, custom strftime format, %.S subsecond, -s elapsed, -i incremental, -i/-s mutex error, -m monotonic, -r relative syslog, -r passthrough, invalid flag, TZ environment\n- etc.\n\nWait, let me reconsider this. The issue mentions implementing cmd/ts per prd004-ts R1-R8, and the planning constitution says to split implementation and tests into separate tasks. So R9 is the test requirement—the implementation task handles R1-R8, while the test task covers the differential tests in R9.\n\nI need to write cmd/ts/ts_test.go with differential tests per prd004-ts R9 and test-rel01.0, reading from the product requirements, test suite spec, the difftest utilities, and the main binary to understand what's being tested. I'll use RunDiffTests with TimestampNormalizer for wall-clock tests, set LC_ALL=C and TZ in test environments, and point to the build binary.\n\nFor the binary path question—I should follow the pattern from prd001-testutils AC1, which says a cmd/ package defines a []DiffTest slice and calls RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, tests). The test needs to build or locate the binary, find the reference binary in PATH, and call RunDiffTests with those paths.\n\nI'm thinking I should use TestMain to build the binary before running tests, then define the test cases and call RunDiffTests with the built binary and the reference binary from PATH. tests run. Let me include this.\n\nActually, looking at prd001-testutils R2.1:\n> Must accept two binary paths: the Go binary under test and the GNU reference binary.\n\nSo RunDiffTests takes the binary paths. The test likely needs to build the binary first or use `go run`.\n\nFor the test task, I should specify:\n- Use `os/exec` to find the reference binary (ts)\n- Build the Go binary via `go build` in TestMain or use a helper\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML task definition with the test file creation, required reading from the specs, and laying out the requirements that map each of the 12 test cases from the test suite to individual requirement IDs. mental_and_elapsed_mutually_exclusive, monotonic_clock_mode, relative_mode_syslog_timestamp, relative_mode_no_timestamp_passthrough, invalid_flag, and tz_environment_respected. I need to apply the TimestampNormalizer to test cases with wall-clock timestamps, while using nil normalization for deterministic outputs. Each test must set LC_ALL=C, with tz_environment_respected and elapsed mode tests also setting the appropriate timezone variables. I'm setting up TestMain to build the Go binary and locate the reference ts binary, skipping if it's not found, then calling RunDiffTests with a single TestTs function where each test case name matches the test-rel01.0 identifiers for clear failure reporting.\n\nFor the incremental_and_elapsed_mutually_exclusive and invalid_flag cases, I'll set non-zero exit codes and verify stderr contains usage strings without normalizing output. The design approach uses TimestampNormalizer for wall-clock timestamps, builds the binary fresh in TestMain to test current source, and stores the path in a package variable for test access.\n\nFor stdin handling, DiffTest.Stdin needs to be a []byte—using byte slice literals for multi-line input and empty []byte for no input. The acceptance criteria require all 12 test cases from test-rel01.0 to be present as named DiffTest entries with exact name matching, tests should skip gracefully when the reference binary isn't available, and go test ./cmd/ts/... must pass completely when both binaries are present.\n\nNow I'm counting through the test cases in the YAML to verify against the 12 mentioned in test-rel01.0—I see default_format_three_lines, default_format_empty_stdin, default_format_partial_last_line, custom_strftime_format, subsecond_format_dotS, elapsed_since_start_mode, incremental_mode, incremental_and_elapsed_mutually_exclusive, and monotonic_clock... but I need to finish the count to make sure I have exactly 12.\n\nLooking at the dependency structure, both pkg/testutils and cmd/ts are already open issues, so this test task depends on both of them being completed first. Since I can only reference one dependency and these aren't part of my current batch, I'll set the dependency to -1 and note in the required_reading that those implementations must come first. Set `dependency` to the index of the crumb that must be completed first, or `-1` if there are no dependencies.\n\nSince I'm only proposing 1 task and it has no dependency on other tasks within this batch (the dependencies are on existing open issues, which the stitch agent must trust are already done), I set dependency to -1.\n\nBut the required_reading should include the files that will be created by those other tasks:\n- `pkg/testutils/difftest.go` (from issue 25b)\n- `cmd/ts/main.go` (from issue g8q)\n\nThe stitch agent will need to read these to understand the API. But since these may not exist yet on the current branch, I should be careful.\n\nActually, looking at the architecture, the working branch contains specifications only—the generated Go code lives at the generation-merged tags. So these files don't exist on the working branch, but the stitch agent is supposed to implement them based on the issues. The ordering matters: issue 25b and g8q must be resolved before this test task is implemented. Since I can't express that dependency directly (they're not in my task list), I should note it in the required_reading and description.\n\nLet me also reconsider whether this task is well-defined enough. The stitch agent needs to know the pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests API, what DiffTest looks like, what TimestampNormalizer does, and the 12/13 test cases—all of which should be documented in the required_reading.\n\nWhen the stitch agent runs, it will read the files listed in required_reading. If those files don't exist, it will fail, so the task assumes pkg/testutils and cmd/ts already exist. Given the constraints say \"Do NOT exceed 1 tasks\", I'll propose just this one task.\n\nNow I'm looking at the DiffTest structure more carefully. The spec requires a slice of Normalize functions that are applied in order, not a single function. I also need to add a Name field for the subtest name since R3.6 requires running each DiffTest as a named subtest with t.Run. I'm settling on referencing the DiffTest API from pkg/testutils as defined by the PRD rather than over-specifying the structure myself. For the test file, I'll plan around 12-13 test cases with DiffTest struct literals, keeping the implementation clean and aligned with the actual API contract.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72940,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72940},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool